diff --git a/SOFT_DELETE.md b/SOFT_DELETE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d783d4a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/SOFT_DELETE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,110 @@
+# Soft Delete + Audit Log — Customer-Data Recovery Net
+
+Reversible deletes and an audit trail for ProBuild's customer data. Added after the
+June 2026 prod data-loss event (see memory `data_loss_june2026_reimport`), which had
+**no in-app recovery** and **no record of what was deleted**. A prod-DB guard for seed
+scripts already exists (`src/lib/db-guard.ts`); this is the recovery layer.
+
+## Covered models
+`Client`, `Lead`, `Project`, `Estimate`, plus the estimate children `EstimateItem`
+and `EstimatePaymentSchedule`.
+
+- `Client`, `Lead`, `Project`, `Estimate` carry `deletedAt DateTime?`, `deletedById String?`,
+  and `deleteBatchId String?`.
+- `EstimateItem`, `EstimatePaymentSchedule` carry `deletedAt DateTime?` + `deleteBatchId String?`
+  (they are always cascade-soft-deleted with their parent estimate; the actor lives on the
+  parent and in the audit log).
+- `deleteBatchId` groups one cascade so restore reverses exactly that batch (see Cascade rules).
+
+## How reads are filtered (the auto-filter extension)
+`src/lib/prisma-soft-delete-extension.ts` is a Prisma Client extension applied in
+`src/lib/prisma.ts`. It injects `where: { deletedAt: null }` into every read
+(`findMany`, `findFirst`, `findFirstOrThrow`, `findUnique`, `findUniqueOrThrow`,
+`count`, `aggregate`, `groupBy`) on the covered models. Because every part of the app
+imports the same `prisma` singleton, soft-deleted rows disappear from all lists with no
+per-query edits. (`findUnique` relies on `extendedWhereUnique`, GA since Prisma 5.)
+
+### Escape hatches (to read deleted rows)
+The delete/restore actions and the admin Trash/Audit UI must see deleted rows:
+1. **`prismaBase`** — the un-extended client exported from `src/lib/prisma.ts`. Reads
+   are NOT filtered. Used by `src/lib/soft-delete.ts` and the Trash UI. **Do not use it
+   for normal app reads.**
+2. **Explicit `deletedAt` in `where`** — if a caller already constrains `deletedAt`
+   (e.g. `{ where: { deletedAt: { not: null } } }`), the extension detects it and skips
+   injection.
+
+### Known limitation — nested relation reads
+Prisma extensions only intercept **top-level** operations, not nested relation reads
+(`include`/`select` of a relation). The cascade rules below are designed so a
+soft-deleted child almost always sits under a soft-deleted (hidden) parent. The two
+exceptions are handled with an explicit relation filter:
+- An **Estimate** deleted under a live Lead/Project → the shared `safeEstimateInclude`
+  and the `getProjects`/`getProject` estimate includes carry `where: { deletedAt: null }`.
+- A **Lead/Project** deleted under a live Client → `getClients`/`getClient` filter the
+  `leads`/`projects` includes with `where: { deletedAt: null }`.
+
+When adding a NEW query that nests `estimates`, `leads`, or `projects` under a covered
+parent, add `where: { deletedAt: null }` to that include.
+
+## Cascade rules
+Each top-level deletion gets a unique `deleteBatchId` (UUID) stamped on the parent and
+**every** row cascaded with it (alongside `deletedAt`). **Restore reverses only the rows
+in that batch**, so an independently-deleted sibling (a different batch) is never
+resurrected by restoring an unrelated ancestor — even if the two deletions happened in the
+same millisecond. (Estimate children are 1:1 with their estimate, so restore scopes them
+by FK; the batch id disambiguates siblings at the estimate/lead/project level.)
+
+| Action (server) | Soft-deletes | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| `deleteEstimate` | Estimate + its EstimateItems + EstimatePaymentSchedules | Keeps existing guards (protected statuses; blocks if linked Expenses/TimeEntries). Budget/Expenses left intact (out of scope, reversible). |
+| `deleteLead` | Lead + its Estimates (+children) | Blocked if **any** project links to the lead — live **or** trashed (checked via `prismaBase`) — and blocked for `Won` leads. Contracts left intact. |
+| `deleteProjects` | Project + its Estimates (+children) | The linked **Lead is left live** — a lead may exist without a project. |
+| `deleteClient` (API `DELETE /api/clients/[id]`) | Client + **all** its Leads + Projects + their Estimates (+children) | Cascading both leads and projects keeps the invariant intact (no live lead-less project). |
+
+### Invariant: every project has a lead (`project_every_project_has_lead`)
+- `deleteLead` refuses to soft-delete a lead that any project (incl. trashed) points to,
+  so a restored project always finds its lead.
+- `restoreProject` additionally restores the project's lead if that lead is soft-deleted
+  (e.g. the whole client was trashed), so a restored project is never lead-less.
+
+## Audit log
+`AuditLog` model: `id`, `entity`, `entityId`, `action` (CREATE|UPDATE|DELETE|RESTORE),
+`actorId`, `actorEmail`, `snapshot Json?`, `createdAt`.
+
+- **DELETE** writes one row per user-initiated deletion with a **full snapshot of the row
+  and its cascaded children** (Decimals/Dates JSON-serialized), so the record is
+  recoverable even after a later hard purge. Written **inside the delete transaction**
+  (atomic).
+- **RESTORE** writes a row per restored top-level record (atomic with the restore).
+- **CREATE/UPDATE** are recorded best-effort (`recordAuditSafe`, non-blocking) at the
+  primary entry points (`createClient`, `createLead`, `createProject`, client `PUT`).
+- The actor comes from the next-auth session via `getAuditActor()` (`getSessionOrDev`).
+
+## Admin Trash / Audit UI
+`/settings/data-recovery` (ADMIN-only; non-admins are redirected). Two tabs:
+- **Trash** — top-level deleted records with a **Restore** button (wired to
+  `restoreTrashItem`). Children that will be restored via a parent are hidden so each row
+  maps 1:1 to a user-initiated deletion.
+- **Audit History** — recent who/what/when entries.
+
+Restore server actions (`restoreClient/Lead/Project/Estimate`, `restoreTrashItem`) and the
+list getters (`getTrashItems`, `getAuditLogEntries`) are all ADMIN-gated.
+
+## Seed / reset paths are unchanged
+Soft delete lives in the **action layer**. The test-DB resets in `prisma/seed.ts` and
+`/api/seed` still hard-`deleteMany({})` (they are already prod-guarded by
+`src/lib/db-guard.ts`). The estimate editor's internal item replacement in `saveEstimate`
+also remains a hard delete — it replaces line items during an edit, it is not a
+user-facing "delete".
+
+## Migration
+Additive + idempotent SQL in `C:\Users\jat00\AppData\Local\Temp\apply_schema.ps1`
+(nullable columns + `AuditLog` table + indexes). It **fails closed against prod** unless
+`ALLOW_PROD_MIGRATION=true`. Apply against a non-prod/branch DB for development, then via
+the normal deploy flow for prod. `DATABASE_URL` must keep `?pgbouncer=true`.
+
+Apply, then regenerate the client via **PowerShell**:
+```
+powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "C:\Users\jat00\AppData\Local\Temp\apply_schema.ps1"
+powershell -Command "cd '<repo>'; node_modules\.bin\prisma generate"
+```
